WEBVTT

00:00:00.246 --> 00:00:01.222
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Part 1

00:00:02.183 --> 00:00:06.542
<v><b>Emma Barnett:</b></v> If we just go back to the start, it all began when you were in your house,

00:00:06.542 --> 00:00:09.060
with your mum, in 1985.

00:00:09.163 --> 00:00:14.067
<v><b>Anthony Ray Hinton:</b></v> Well, um, that particular morning, like any other morning,

00:00:14.092 --> 00:00:19.603
I woke up and had breakfast and went outside and laid on up and the –

00:00:19.852 --> 00:00:25.447
the day, it got hotter and hotter in Alabama – and it was in July and…

00:00:25.914 --> 00:00:28.445
I goes out and I fire up the old lawnmower,

00:00:28.445 --> 00:00:31.765
and about 25 minutes into cutting the grass I just happen to look up,

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:34.506
and there stood two white gentlemen that I’d never seen before.

00:00:34.855 --> 00:00:37.938
I cut the lawnmower off and I said, ‘Can I help you?’

00:00:38.136 --> 00:00:42.069
and one of ’em replied, ‘Yes, we’re looking for Anthony Ray Hinton.’

00:00:42.251 --> 00:00:45.129
I said, ‘That would be me. Again, how can I help you?’

00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:49.244
and he said, ‘We are detectives from the Birmingham Police Department.

00:00:49.569 --> 00:00:51.325
We have a warrant for your arrest.’

00:00:51.491 --> 00:00:54.176
And I kept asking the detective, ‘Why am I being arrested?’

00:00:54.446 --> 00:00:58.427
They never would reply, and I said, ‘Detective, why are y’all arresting me?’

00:00:58.452 --> 00:01:02.755
and he finally turned around and he said, ‘We gonna charge you with first-degree robbery,

00:01:02.755 --> 00:01:05.085
first-degree kidnapping, first-degree attempted murder.’

00:01:05.378 --> 00:01:07.970
I said, ‘Well, you got the wrong guy. I ain’t done none of that.’

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:10.052
He said, ‘Let me tell you something, right now,

00:01:10.329 --> 00:01:12.449
I don’t care whether you did it or didn’t do it,

00:01:12.474 --> 00:01:15.209
but I want you to know that you gonna be convicted of it.’

00:01:15.349 --> 00:01:16.718
He said, ‘I’m gonna make sure of it.’

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:18.440
And I got convicted.

00:01:18.931 --> 00:01:22.501
The judge proudly stood up that day and said,

00:01:22.526 --> 00:01:27.410
‘Anthony Ray Hinton, you have been found guilty by a jury of your peers,

00:01:28.203 --> 00:01:31.787
and it’s the order of this court that I sentence you to death,

00:01:32.581 --> 00:01:34.871
and may God have mercy on your soul.’

00:01:36.078 --> 00:01:39.724
I had no feelings, I, I just felt numb,

00:01:39.749 --> 00:01:43.165
I felt alive but yet dead at the same time.

00:01:43.601 --> 00:01:48.362
I kept wondering how a innocent man that was out cutting grass,

00:01:48.378 --> 00:01:52.199
how do he go from cutting grass to all of a sudden

00:01:52.199 --> 00:01:58.117
his whole world is turned upside down, and now the judge is saying,

00:01:58.347 --> 00:02:03.782
‘I sentence you to death.’ And I kept saying, ‘How did I get here?

00:02:03.964 --> 00:02:07.968
How am I gonna be killed for something that I didn’t do?

00:02:08.604 --> 00:02:14.126
What did I do so bad in this world that I deserve this?’

00:02:14.943 --> 00:02:15.565
<v><b>Emma Barnett:</b></v>I was gonna say,

00:02:15.564 --> 00:02:20.576
how long were you angry for in those first years of prison, then?

00:02:20.656 --> 00:02:22.469
<v><b>Anthony Ray Hinton:</b></v> Three long years.

00:02:22.494 --> 00:02:25.384
I didn’t speak to another human being for three years.

00:02:25.692 --> 00:02:29.460
Every time one of the guards would ask me anything,

00:02:29.992 --> 00:02:34.927
I’d just get a pencil out, or a pen, or whatever I had,

00:02:35.284 --> 00:02:38.922
and write my response. I didn’t wanna talk to nobody.

00:02:39.208 --> 00:02:41.800
I was just sitting there feeding off hate.

00:02:42.322 --> 00:02:44.160
<v><b>Emma Barnett:</b></v>What happened after three years?

00:02:44.398 --> 00:02:47.113
<v><b>Anthony Ray Hinton:</b></v> Well, after those three years…

00:02:47.939 --> 00:02:52.515
I woke up to the sound of a grown man crying and weeping.

00:02:53.349 --> 00:02:56.094
This is a man that I had lived by for three years

00:02:56.093 --> 00:02:59.284
and never asked him his name or where he was from.

00:03:00.315 --> 00:03:04.057
But, my mother had taught me compassion at a very early age

00:03:04.082 --> 00:03:11.401
and she told me that no matter what one does in life, he or she deserved compassion.

00:03:11.655 --> 00:03:16.397
And so, that compassion made me, for the first time, er, holler out,

00:03:16.802 --> 00:03:20.982
and I said, ‘Hey, over there!’ I said, ‘Is something wrong?’

00:03:21.212 --> 00:03:26.504
And it took this man a while to reply and finally he replied and he said,

00:03:26.504 --> 00:03:29.714
‘Well, I just got word my mother passed.’

00:03:30.526 --> 00:03:33.054
And I told him how sorry I was to hear that.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:37.659
And I told him that if I could do anything, let me know.

00:03:38.096 --> 00:03:46.788
I sit back on that little small bed and that’s when I thanked God that my mother was still alive,

00:03:47.018 --> 00:03:52.579
and I think that was the beginning of me coming back to – to human form,

00:03:53.103 --> 00:04:00.421
to realize that, although I was in a five-by-seven, I had something to be thankful for…

00:04:00.945 --> 00:04:03.522
<v><b>Emma Barnett:</b></v> You did have a friend, as I understand it,

00:04:03.522 --> 00:04:05.641
who came to see you throughout this time.

00:04:06.124 --> 00:04:08.256
<v><b>Anthony Ray Hinton:</b></v> Oh, you talking ’bout Lester!

00:04:08.415 --> 00:04:09.027
<v><b>Emma Barnett:</b></v> Yes.

00:04:09.052 --> 00:04:12.577
<v><b>Anthony Ray Hinton:</b></v> Oh yes, I smile every time I see Lester today.

00:04:12.649 --> 00:04:18.400
I talk to Lester every day. Lester came to see me 30 straight years.

00:04:18.551 --> 00:04:22.922
Lester came a total of 10,999 visits.

00:04:23.223 --> 00:04:28.077
Er, rain, sleet, snow, whatever, Lester always came…

00:04:28.323 --> 00:04:32.440
Lester was four and I was six at the time we met each other.

00:04:32.829 --> 00:04:37.493
Two little kids that just – our mothers told us to go out and play,

00:04:37.723 --> 00:04:41.362
and our friendship have lasted for 59 years,

00:04:41.387 --> 00:04:47.876
and Lester and I don’t agree on everything, but Lester and I have a bond that is unbreakable.